@AlexWaygood , I reused comments of @erlend-aasland in #93370 

> The sqlite3.version and sqlite3.version_info attributes have historically created some confusion, as they are sometimes mistaken for the SQLite 3 version number. **They used to reflect the pysqlite[1](https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/93370#user-content-fn-1-64a844a6f8fbcb94eb70df26d647a862) version number, until the external pysqlite package stopped upstreaming changes to the stdlib sqlite3 module[2](https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/93370#user-content-fn-2-64a844a6f8fbcb94eb70df26d647a862), but today they carry no meaning or practical value**[3](https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/93370#user-content-fn-3-64a844a6f8fbcb94eb70df26d647a862).

In order to avoid future frustration, I suggest to deprecate sqlite3.version and sqlite3.version_info in 3.12, and remove them in 3.14.
